derivations.claim:
The fold set from step 1.2 is a compact zero-dimensional transverse preimage in the two-dimensional cylinder, hence finite. Near a fold $(s_0,x_0)$, the equation $h_x(s,x)=0$ has $h_x=h_{xx}=0$, $h_{xxx}
e0$, and $h_{xs}
e0$. By [F5], solve it locally as $s=\psi(x)$; differentiating gives $\psi'(x_0)=0$ and $\psi''(x_0)=-h_{xxx}/h_{xs}
e0$. Thus on one side of $s_0$ there are exactly two nondegenerate critical points and on the other side none. Between these finitely many fold values, $h_x=0$ is a finite union of smooth branches by the implicit function theorem. At the birth, the two new critical values are distinct away from the fold: $h_x$ has constant nonzero sign between its two roots, so $h(s,x_+)-h(s,x_-)=\int_{x_-}^{x_+}h_x(s,x)\,dx
e0$.

